OPAL

Performance-first remote desktop for Linux, Apple Silicon macOS, and Windows.
Uses signed rendezvous, end-to-end encrypted direct UDP, encrypted relay fallback, and Tailscale when available.

BUILD
=====

Linux:

  make -j"$(nproc)"
  sudo make install

Apple Silicon macOS 13+:

  brew install pkg-config sdl3 openssl@3 ffmpeg git
  make
  make install

Do not use sudo make install on macOS.

Windows 10+ from MSYS2 UCRT64:

  pacman -S --needed mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-toolchain mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-pkgconf mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-SDL3 mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-openssl mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-ffmpeg make git
  make
  make verify
  make install

Windows headless support additionally needs Visual Studio MSBuild and the Windows Driver Kit. When they are present, make install builds and installs the OPAL IddCx display driver automatically. The driver supports Windows 10 1803+ and Windows 11. Driver installation requires the normal Windows elevation prompt and may require a restart. Without MSBuild/WDK, monitor-attached OPAL remains usable.

  make windows-headless

HEADLESS HOSTING
================

OPAL prefers the real desktop while a usable physical display exists. If no usable display exists, or the active physical display becomes unavailable during a session, OPAL rebuilds only the media generation around an OPAL-owned virtual display. The authenticated peer remains connected and HOST_META is refreshed when the display geometry changes.

Linux:

  Existing KDE/Wayland: KWin virtual output + direct PipeWire node capture.
  TTY/no graphical session: managed KWin + Plasma + Xwayland session, then direct PipeWire capture.
  Input: KWin fake-input for OPAL virtual desktops; /dev/uinput for the normal physical path.
  Required for managed headless mode: kwin_wayland, plasmashell, Xwayland, PipeWire, a user D-Bus session, and XDG_RUNTIME_DIR.
  Enable user lingering for hosting after logout/TTY boot when needed:

    sudo loginctl enable-linger $USER

macOS:

  Existing display: ScreenCaptureKit.
  Headless fallback: OPAL creates a CoreGraphics virtual display and targets it explicitly with ScreenCaptureKit.
  CGVirtualDisplay is an undocumented macOS runtime API. OPAL validates the classes/selectors before use and fails cleanly when the installed macOS runtime is incompatible, but Apple does not provide a public equivalent for creating a full headless GUI display.

Windows:

  Existing display: DXGI Desktop Duplication + D3D11.
  Headless fallback: OPAL IddCx virtual monitor + D3D11 capture path.
  OPAL detects physical-output loss using Windows display topology/session power state and read-only DDC/MCCS power information where the monitor supports it. A static desktop alone never triggers failover.
  When physical capture becomes unavailable, OPAL prefers the IddCx display on the next media generation and falls back to physical capture again if virtual creation fails.

RUN
===

  opal
  opal <connection-code>
  opal select
  opal list
  opal new
  opal remove
  opal doctor
  opal stop
  opal restart
  OPAL_DEBUG=1 opal <connection-code>

Windows uses opal.exe for the same commands.

BACKENDS
========

Linux
  capture      PipeWire/libportal physical capture; KWin direct PipeWire virtual capture
  encode       in-process FFmpeg/hardware H.264
  presentation SDL3
  input        /dev/uinput or KWin fake-input
  clipboard    wl-clipboard with SDL3 fallback
  service      systemd user service

macOS
  capture      ScreenCaptureKit physical/explicit virtual display
  encode       VideoToolbox H.264 low latency
  presentation SDL3
  input        Accessibility-authorized CGEvent helper
  clipboard    NSPasteboard
  service      launchd LaunchAgent

Windows
  capture      DXGI Desktop Duplication or OPAL IddCx virtual display
  encode       D3D11 Video Processor BGRA->NV12 + Media Foundation H.264
  decode       FFmpeg/D3D11VA with software fallback
  presentation D3D11 swapchain with SDL3 fallback
  input        SendInput
  clipboard    CF_UNICODETEXT
  service      per-user Run registry + detached host daemon

Tailscale is optional on every platform. LAN discovery, signed rendezvous, direct UDP, and relay fallback remain available without it.
